      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "During the Civil War, a Home Guard post called Camp Rio Frio was built to shield the community from Apaches and bandits.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tilden%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After the war, the town earned the nickname Dog Town for its reputation as a lawless, bandit‑filled place.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tilden%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1877 the abandoned Boot Hill Cemetery was replaced by Hill Top Cemetery, which remained neglected for more than fifty years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tilden%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town sits roughly 70 miles south of San Antonio, placing it in the heart of Texas’s southern plains.",
